WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.846 --> 00:00:02.146
An ordinary street.

2
00:00:02.958 --> 00:00:05.919
At the end stands a distribution substation;

3
00:00:06.070 --> 00:00:08.787
under the pavement a cable runs to every home.

4
00:00:09.842 --> 00:00:13.058
Electricity in that cable is like water in a pipe.

5
00:00:13.810 --> 00:00:18.547
The voltage is the push, in volts: 230 volts in a Dutch home.

6
00:00:19.571 --> 00:00:22.265
The current is how much flows, in amperes.

7
00:00:22.991 --> 00:00:25.615
Current heats cables and blows fuses.

8
00:00:26.619 --> 00:00:31.507
Power is how fast energy is used: voltage times current, in watts.

9
00:00:32.215 --> 00:00:34.270
A thousand watts is one kilowatt.

10
00:00:35.307 --> 00:00:41.228
So a 2,000 watt kettle on 230 volts draws about 8.7 amps.

11
00:00:42.550 --> 00:00:44.443
Power is the reading of the moment.

12
00:00:45.191 --> 00:00:48.965
Energy is power times time, added up, in kilowatt hours.

13
00:00:49.982 --> 00:00:52.037
If the kettle runs for 3 minutes,

14
00:00:52.176 --> 00:00:57.969
that is 2 kilowatts times 0.05 hours: 0.1 kilowatt hours.

15
00:00:58.976 --> 00:01:03.887
An average family uses about 2,700 kilowatt hours a year.

16
00:01:04.604 --> 00:01:12.395
Spread over the 8,760 hours of a year, that is on average only 0.31 kilowatts.

17
00:01:13.393 --> 00:01:14.856
You pay per kilowatt hour,

18
00:01:14.995 --> 00:01:19.744
but cables and transformers must carry the highest power at any one moment:

19
00:01:19.953 --> 00:01:21.868
the grid is built for kilowatts.

20
00:01:23.164 --> 00:01:25.498
The grid runs on alternating current:

21
00:01:25.695 --> 00:01:29.132
the voltage swings back and forth 50 times a second,

22
00:01:29.236 --> 00:01:30.246
50 hertz.

23
00:01:30.932 --> 00:01:35.774
Solar panels and batteries give direct current, which flows one way only.

24
00:01:36.753 --> 00:01:41.223
AC won because a transformer can easily step its voltage up and down.

25
00:01:41.967 --> 00:01:45.891
Here it turns medium voltage into low voltage for the street.

26
00:01:46.963 --> 00:01:52.129
The street cable is three-phase: three phases, each a third of a cycle apart,

27
00:01:52.292 --> 00:01:54.440
plus a neutral as the return wire.

28
00:01:55.450 --> 00:02:01.151
Between a phase and neutral there are 230 volts, between two phases 400.

29
00:02:01.879 --> 00:02:05.176
Heat pumps and car chargers often use all three.

30
00:02:06.453 --> 00:02:13.001
A common house connection is 3 times 25 amps: at most about 17 kilovolt amperes,

31
00:02:13.187 --> 00:02:15.962
the unit for what a cable or fuse must carry.

32
00:02:16.937 --> 00:02:21.906
A day in one home: mostly it asks little, often less than the yearly average.

33
00:02:22.911 --> 00:02:25.001
But at six in the evening the oven,

34
00:02:25.222 --> 00:02:30.145
the kettle and the dishwasher run together: 3 plus 2 plus 2 is 7 kilowatts.

35
00:02:31.149 --> 00:02:34.620
If an electric car also charges at 11 kilowatts,

36
00:02:34.795 --> 00:02:38.731
the house draws 18 kilowatts: more than the connection allows.

37
00:02:39.442 --> 00:02:40.951
The main fuse may blow.

38
00:02:42.276 --> 00:02:43.681
Now the whole district.

39
00:02:44.465 --> 00:02:48.052
This distribution substation has a 400 kilovolt

40
00:02:48.168 --> 00:02:51.872
ampere transformer and feeds 150 homes.

41
00:02:52.886 --> 00:02:57.949
Those homes do not all peak at once: one cooks at six, another at half past seven.

42
00:02:58.640 --> 00:03:03.713
That is simultaneity: how much of everyone’s maximum happens at the same moment.

43
00:03:04.759 --> 00:03:09.554
The peak load is the highest load of the year, usually on a cold winter evening.

44
00:03:10.288 --> 00:03:15.176
Traditionally planners counted 1 to 1.5 kilovolt amperes per home,

45
00:03:15.316 --> 00:03:17.626
far below the 17 of the connection.

46
00:03:18.624 --> 00:03:24.243
150 times 1.5 is 225 kilovolt amperes:

47
00:03:24.394 --> 00:03:28.527
at its busiest moment the transformer is 56 percent loaded.

48
00:03:29.881 --> 00:03:32.447
Why kilovolt amperes and not kilowatts?

49
00:03:33.173 --> 00:03:35.936
A cable or transformer carries all the current,

50
00:03:36.076 --> 00:03:40.232
also a part that motors and electronics only push back and forth.

51
00:03:40.970 --> 00:03:42.874
That total is the apparent power.

52
00:03:43.886 --> 00:03:46.603
The useful part is the power in kilowatts.

53
00:03:47.300 --> 00:03:50.621
The ratio of the two is the power factor, or cos phi.

54
00:03:51.321 --> 00:03:54.049
For homes usually 0.9 to 1.

55
00:03:55.060 --> 00:04:01.980
So a 400 kilovolt ampere transformer delivers about 360 to 400 kilowatts.

56
00:04:03.298 --> 00:04:06.259
Now 50 homes get a 3 kilowatt heat pump.

57
00:04:07.005 --> 00:04:11.196
On a cold evening they all run at once: a simultaneity close to one.

58
00:04:11.904 --> 00:04:17.186
Each runs at about 80 percent: together 120 kilovolt amperes more.

59
00:04:18.216 --> 00:04:22.070
And 40 homes charge a car at 11 kilowatts after work,

60
00:04:22.245 --> 00:04:24.753
about 30 percent at the same moment.

61
00:04:25.538 --> 00:04:28.916
Another 132 kilovolt amperes.

62
00:04:29.935 --> 00:04:38.840
The new peak: 225 plus 120 plus 132 is 477 kilovolt amperes.

63
00:04:39.702 --> 00:04:42.999
That is 119 percent of the transformer.

64
00:04:44.019 --> 00:04:47.955
The yearly energy grows far less, but the peak more than doubles.

65
00:04:49.279 --> 00:04:52.135
On a sunny afternoon a peak comes the other way:

66
00:04:52.344 --> 00:04:54.724
every roof feeds back at the same time.

67
00:04:55.728 --> 00:05:00.314
The inverter turns the panels’ direct current into alternating current.

68
00:05:01.062 --> 00:05:03.837
Here that box hangs outside on the end wall.

69
00:05:04.814 --> 00:05:09.097
All that feed-in pushes the voltage up, most at the end of a long cable.

70
00:05:10.142 --> 00:05:17.096
Above about 253 volts, the European upper limit of 230 volts plus 10 percent,

71
00:05:17.293 --> 00:05:18.767
the inverter switches off.

72
00:05:19.495 --> 00:05:24.440
Panels that keep dropping out are often a voltage problem, not a broken inverter.

73
00:05:25.768 --> 00:05:30.354
What experts argue about: the simultaneity of heat pumps and chargers.

74
00:05:31.401 --> 00:05:35.592
Assume too low, and the grid overloads in the first cold week.

75
00:05:36.318 --> 00:05:39.580
Too high, and streets are dug up years too early.

76
00:05:40.584 --> 00:05:45.577
Smart charging, which spreads car charging over the night, can cut the peak a lot.

77
00:05:46.355 --> 00:05:48.155
But only if people allow it.

78
00:05:49.179 --> 00:05:52.546
So always ask: which simultaneity is this plan based on,

79
00:05:52.767 --> 00:05:54.474
and where does that number come from?
